*** In closing: ***

Current human behavior (exponential population growth driving
ever-increasing demands on the Earth's resources) is not sustainable.
We can wait to change our behavior until the alternative is death, and
accept a radically reduced quality of life in return for the dubious
enjoyment of conspicuous consumption (read Veblen for a definition),
or we can change before we are forced and have a much better life with
the physical space and the leisure time to explore our fascinating
universe.

I do not believe coercion to be a good long-term solution to this problem,
just as it is not a long-term solution to firearm deaths or economic
instability.

I believe that the challenge of boundless expansion requires
that we know our current limitations so as to effectively transcend them.

I believe that drooling over nonexistent technology will get us
nowhere, and that we must actively seek ways of life that will allow
us the time and tools to explore and invent new technology.  (Hint:
That's not working for someone else 60 hours a week.)

I believe this is possible, otherwise I wouldn't bother to say it.

