From: plaz@netcom.com (Geoff Dale)
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 1994 00:53:37 -0800
Subject: [#94-1-615] LEGAL: PPL proposals, my $.02 

My comments on the various PPL proposals floating around.

1> Mike Price's InterPPL negotiation framework.

Simon! replies to Mike Price:
>> My own proposal for the inter-PPL charter is simplicity itself.  Anyone
>> can file charges against anyone, but they are judged solely against the
>> defendant's PPL charter or etiquette code.  A natural Schelling point
>> I feel, although more complex solutions must be possible.
>
>One last time: I agree.

I'm not sure I do, entirely.

I think a PPL should be allowed to protect it's members from things that
are illegal in that law system. It should be up to the PPL to decide
whether the are going to handle going after a non-client for violating his
own PPL's rules, or press charges against it's own client for something
that is not illegal in the "offended" party's PPL.

In my PPL's case:
If a slanderous/libelous act were committed against one of my clients by a
member of another PPL (and my client wished to "press charges"), I would
feel obligated to follow up on it. If this action were not "illegal" in the
other PPL, then the inter-PPL negotiation would begin in earnest.

On the other hand:
Alice, a member of PPL-1 flames Bob, who is a member of PPL-A. Can he press
charges? If he does, who should he contact, Simon! or myself?

The answer is: Its a free market, try it and find out what works. I
probably wouldn't launch into an inter-PPL negotiation to prosecute
something that wasn't illegal, but if Bob flamed back and Alice pressed
charges, we obviously have a problem.

But, I do agree with the basic principle. If an act is illegal in both
PPLs, then the interPPL negotiation is a no-brainer. I'm not sure how a
truly free market would handle rogue PPLs that protect their members from
anything no matter how attrocious said members act.

2> Moderation as a "Punishment"

I think this is a generally good idea. But I don't think there should be an
"official" moderator. This could lead to problems if the moderator and
moderatee have a personality conflict of thier own.

People could start Moderation Agencies. In the case of a judgment against a
person, the convicted member could be offered the option of being moderated
instead of being kicked off the list. The choice of moderation services
should be agreeable to all parties, the litigator, the PPL, the convict and
the moderation service. If it can't be worked out, the convict should have
to leave the list for the proscribed period of time.

The moderation should, IMHO, be sensitive to what the person was
"convicted" for.

3> Mandatory LEGAL: prefixes and forced "::exclude thread LEGAL:"

While I agree with general proposal, let's make it "recommended" prefix and
excluded by default, shall we? 

The "default" exclude should also be mentioned in the welcome message.

Would the exclude "decay" after a period of time?

4> The default PPL

I had proposed earlier that the default PPL be deliberately brain-damaged,
perhaps receive only (at least for a free trial period). It has the effect
of squelching newbies (although clued in newbies could get past it quite
quickly).

There is also the "outlaw" system that Ray proposed. The default state
could be no protection.



