>Subject: [#94-1-592] Pancreatic Rationalism
>
>Tim Starr says of PanCritRat--
>
>> 1) ...It seems to fail to address the question of why
>> it should be considered "credible," on its own terms.
>
>It says, don't expect justification for *anything*, only hold things open 
>to criticism.

So what?  Why not?  Consider my position: I have a "justificationist" philosophy
which seems to escape the criticisms PCR makes of what Kelley calls "Cartesian
Empiricism."  It does present reasons for me to adopt it.  What reason do I
have to prefer PCR?

>Nothing *should* be considered credible on PCR's terms.
>So the way PCR doesn't offer justification for itself isn't contradictory, 
>it's a good example!

My objection wasn't that it was inconsistent, but that it was irrelevant.  I
have no reason to adopt it, nor does anyone else.  The main reason to adopt
it seems to be a horror of "justificationism."  What if the reasons to be
afraid of justificationism go away when a reconstructed version of it is
developed?

>> 2) ...it doesn't seem to make any difference whether
>> you say that you're more certain of some truths than others or some are more
> credible than others.
>
>I think putting it in personal terms is just a way of stressing that PCR
>doesn't give you any starting point, or even criteria for good evidence in
>criticism, except what you happen to be holding on to at the moment.

Yes, but what if you aren't holding on to any at the moment, or you're holding
on to ones that are incompatible with PCR?

>3) It seems to assume that minds are random-idea generators, failing to address
> the question of the origins of beliefs/awareness/concepts, merely assuming
> that they do.  If this isn't contrary to your experience (it is to mine), then
> I'd be surprised.

>Hmm.  Merely assuming that minds generate ideas doesn't contradict my 
>experience.

You seem to be missing my point: minds undoubtedly generate ideas, but do they
do so AT RANDOM in your experience?  Not in mine, nor that of anyone I'm
familiar with.  There's always some principle in operation.

>PCR doesn't talk much about idea generation, only the importance
>of testing.

Precisely my point.  PCR seems to only address half the problem.  As Rand
pointed out, this is one of the classic mistakes of empiricism:  "How did
the concepts get here?  Somehow."

>There's a lot of randomness in the mental processes I do know
>about, and thinking is "random" in the sense that you can't put definite
>bounds on what somebody might come up with.

That's not randomness, that's infiniteness.  Popper goes so far as to say
that there's no order to how ideas are generated, so unless Bartley or some
other PCR-theorist gainsays him, the default presumption seems to be that
idea-generation is random, which is contradicted by seemingly universal
experience.

>Personally, I'm interested in genesis and justifications of ideas, but I
>think Bartley wanted to put his point in a stark light.

Maybe so, but he seems to have come up with a system which is inherently
incomplete.

